@386[3]. "The serpent's root" = this false principle (of faith alone). ( = the Sensuous. @817[9].)

@544[2]. Under <Scorpion>, see this reference.

@581. "Their tails were like serpents"... "Serpents" = the astutenesses of the sensuous man.

@--[2]. "Serpents," in the Word, = the sensuous man as to cunning, and as to prudence. Fully illustrated. @--[9].

@--. "The serpent was more cunning than any beast..." = the sensuous man; in general, the Sensuous itself. Explained. @763[end].

@--[3]. "Serpents" = the affections of the sensuous man, because they crawl -serpunt-... Explained.

@--[4]. Hence the devil, or Hell, is called "a serpent." Illustrated.

@--[6]. To "tread on serpents" = to despise... the wiles, cunning, and wicked arts of the infernal crew. @--[7]. Illustrated.

@--[12]. "The fiery flying serpents" = sensuous things...

@650[23]. The Gentiles worshipped serpents, because the serpents the Sensuous... and its prudence.

